+- after a 30 fps cap ends (a prerendered movie, the thermal cap), a GPU-bound game stays at 30 fps in a scene that held 60 before the cap; the log shows `frame step cap 30 → 60 fps` and a few frames later `frame step: hold 1 → 2 vsyncs at present … (3 late frames; …)`, with no probe after it. → [The frame step after a 30 fps cap ends](../reference/frame-rate.md#the-frame-step-after-a-30-fps-cap-ends)
+- on a Mac, a GPU-bound game at 60 fps falls to 30 or 20 fps when the scene gets heavier and stays there until a menu or a lighter scene, while the GPU clock sits at its floor (338–400 MHz on an M5 Pro) and the GPU is powered 60–75% of the time; the log shows probes back to hold 1 that fail after about 33 frames (`frame step: hold 2 → 1 vsyncs … (probe, GPU 1.67× its time at hold 1)` then `the probe failed`), and the host's profiler (`@gpu_mhz`) shows the clock rising during each probe and falling back after it. → [A probe's grace lasts while its frames keep getting cheaper](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-probes-grace-lasts-while-its-frames-keep-getting-cheaper)
+- a game whose profile has a frame step (`FRAME_STEP` in its `patches.py`) runs at double speed when packed from another update; the log has no `frame step in whole vsyncs` line, and the frame counter shows 60. → [A derived profile drops the frame step its build lacks](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-derived-profile-drops-the-frame-step-its-build-lacks)
+- a fixed-step game runs at double speed (or slow motion at 30) after `DM_FPS_TARGET` is set, in `Documents/env.txt` or the launcher, on a build its profile's frame step stubs and limiter rows do not know. → [A present cap applies only with a frame step or an engine limit the host writes](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-present-cap-applies-only-with-a-frame-step-or-an-engine-limit-the-host-writes)
+- a game holds 30 fps with spare CPU and GPU time; it asks for present interval 2 once (`the game sets present interval 1 → 2`) and nothing else limits it. With `present_interval display` it runs at 60, but `fps_target` logs `present cap … not applied` and the player's **Frame rate** (30 or 60) does nothing: frames stay at one a display tick. → [A present interval that is the game's only limit, in display mode](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-present-interval-that-is-the-games-only-limit-in-display-mode)
+- selecting 30 FPS changes the engine's interval field, but the screen and frame loop still run near 60 FPS. → [Engine-native frame caps also govern presentation](../reference/frame-rate.md#engine-native-frame-caps-also-govern-presentation)
+- the game always renders at full resolution, or turning off dynamic resolution still allows the native controller to lower it. → [Resolution policy is independent of the frame-rate cap](../reference/frame-rate.md#resolution-policy-is-independent-of-the-frame-rate-cap)
+- slow motion when the GPU is the bottleneck: a game's interval controller that times the GPU never steps up (GitHub issue #9). → [GPU timestamps per submission segment](../reference/frame-rate.md#gpu-timestamps-per-submission-segment)
+- GPU time reads 32 times too small when a game converts raw NVN counter timestamps itself. → [Raw NVN timestamp units](../reference/frame-rate.md#raw-nvn-timestamp-units)
+- a Switch 2 game holds 20 or 30 fps on the Mac although its frame work fits in one vsync: its frame-rate controller raises the present interval (`the game sets present interval 1 → 3` in the log) and never steps back down. → [The Switch 2's system tick is 31.25 MHz](../reference/frame-rate.md#the-switch-2s-system-tick-is-3125-mhz)
+- menus reach 60 fps with `present_interval display`, but entering gameplay returns to 30 fps despite spare GPU time. → [Unity's integer target frame rate](../reference/frame-rate.md#unitys-integer-target-frame-rate)
+- a Unity game holds 30 fps with spare GPU time (about 2 ms a frame) and sets present interval 2 at its title (`the game sets present interval 1 → 2 (from ?+0)`); with `fps_target 60` the log says the present cap is not applied, as the host writes no limit of this main. → [Unity's vSyncCount and targetFrameRate, found through the internal-call table](../reference/frame-rate.md#unitys-vsynccount-and-targetframerate-found-through-the-internal-call-table)
+- the game presents at interval 1, yet every frame takes exactly 33.3 ms of "frame work"; after only the limiter was raised, everything ran twice as fast on the iPhone. → [A render-thread limiter with a constant and a fixed time step](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-render-thread-limiter-with-a-constant-and-a-fixed-time-step)
+- 55 fps with every frame 18.2 ms, the main thread asleep most of the time; every 11th frame stays on screen two vsyncs. → [A frame-end wait polled in 2 ms sleeps](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-frame-end-wait-polled-in-2-ms-sleeps)
+- a Switch 2 game runs its title and menus at 60 fps and its gameplay at 30, asking for present interval 2, with the GPU mostly idle. → [A target frame rate the game steps by](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-target-frame-rate-the-game-steps-by)
+- with a game's target frame rate written to 120 and a 120 Hz display link, the game holds 120 where frames fit, but a scene whose frames take 14 ms runs in slow motion at 72 fps, and on a 60 Hz screen the whole game runs at half speed. → [120 fps follows the frame step](../reference/frame-rate.md#120-fps-follows-the-frame-step)
+- with `DM_FPS_TARGET=120`, a 120 Hz screen and the cap at 120 (`120 fps: cap 0 → 120 fps`), the game holds 60: every trial of hold 1 ends in `hold 1 → 2 … (3 late frames; cost 2.0…)` with the CPU mostly idle and the GPU under 1 ms a frame. → [The display's vsync event follows 120 fps](../reference/frame-rate.md#the-displays-vsync-event-follows-120-fps)
+- an Unreal Engine game holds 30 fps with spare CPU and GPU time and asks for present interval 2; `present_interval display` alone does not help, as the engine's game thread sleeps out its own frame limit. → [Unreal's frame limit and present interval, found by name](../reference/frame-rate.md#unreals-frame-limit-and-present-interval-found-by-name)
+- an Unreal Engine game with `fps_target 60` asks for present interval 1 (`main: Unreal rhi.SyncInterval … 2 → 1 vsyncs`), and its menus reach 60, but its gameplay holds 30.0 fps with spare CPU and GPU time; the game sets no `t.MaxFPS`. → [Unreal's fixed frame rate, found by its reflection entry](../reference/frame-rate.md#unreals-fixed-frame-rate-found-by-its-reflection-entry)
+- a Frostbite game holds 30 fps with spare CPU and GPU time. Raising only the frame limit and the present interval gives 60 frames a second with the game at half speed (the match clock at 3.6 game seconds a second instead of 7.5). → [Frostbite's simulation rate, found by name](../reference/frame-rate.md#frostbites-simulation-rate-found-by-name)
+- the app is active and the game keeps running, but the picture freezes while display-link callbacks stop; the log repeatedly drops queued frames. → [Present thread rescues a silent display link](../reference/frame-rate.md#present-thread-rescues-a-silent-display-link)
+- `[longframe]` lines of 40–180 ms with no new pipelines, a short submit and normal GPU time, which read like CPU hitches in the game: single frames of 60–100 ms, or bursts of 5–12 frames, one of 140–180 ms and the rest about 40 ms. → [Long frames a test run makes itself](../reference/frame-rate.md#long-frames-a-test-run-makes-itself)
+- with the display link driving presents, a presented frame comes out black when three presents are on the GPU at once, or two after a dropped frame; on an M6 the same reuse also logs `[RED] display command buffer error` with `kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorPageFault`. → [A present command allocator is reused only after its own present completes](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-present-command-allocator-is-reused-only-after-its-own-present-completes)
+- "sometimes the speed increases when jumping" (Mario 1.2.1 on the iPhone): in `display` mode interval-2 frames were shown for 16.7 ms and the game visibly jumped ahead. "When pressing B, Link's move pace is speeding up" (Breath of the Wild on the iPhone); `old frames dropped by merging`. → [Frames of a dynamic present interval held on screen and at the acquire](../reference/frame-rate.md#frames-of-a-dynamic-present-interval-held-on-screen-and-at-the-acquire)
+- a loading screen that should take seconds runs for minutes at 60 fps with a few long frames of new pipelines; the log has `the game sets present interval 1 → 0` before it and `0 → 1` after it, and the graphics worker thread spends most of its time in `nvnWindowAcquireTexture` → `mtl_link_wait_ticks`. → [Present interval 0 waits for no vsync](../reference/frame-rate.md#present-interval-0-waits-for-no-vsync)
+- a game the profile upgrades to 60 fps (`fps_target 60`, `present_interval display`) drops to about 25 fps in a window on an M2 Pro, from the first frames and in every scene, with no red line, once `nn::vi::ListFrameHistory` lists the presents (Layer frame history). On an M6 it runs at 54–58 fps with 33 ms frames and empty display-link ticks. The `present interval set by the game` in the `interval mode display` line is 2. → [A game's own present pacing reads the interval the window presents at](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-games-own-present-pacing-reads-the-interval-the-window-presents-at)
+- a game that asks NVN for fewer active swap chain textures than it gave the window gets one frame more queued between its rendering and the screen than it chose (input latency), with a log line `WindowBuilderSetNumActiveTextures n (the host rotates through the SetTextures count)`. → [Swap chain textures a game leaves inactive](../reference/frame-rate.md#swap-chain-textures-a-game-leaves-inactive)
+- Donkey Kong Bananza ran its heavy areas at 15 fps where its GPU took about 46 ms a frame: the frames came every 66.7 ms, and the presentation thread spent 85% of its time in `nvnWindowAcquireTexture` waiting for display-link ticks. → [Odd present intervals on the 30 Hz display link](../reference/frame-rate.md#odd-present-intervals-on-the-30-hz-display-link)
+- with `present_interval display`, `DM_PRESENT_HZ=120` and `DM_PRESENT_LINK=2` (a 120 Hz display link) a game stays at 60 fps although its frame takes 8 ms; the display link logs about two ticks per present. With a requested 90 Hz, truncating the tick conversion instead allows 120 fps (reproduced through the real acquire with simulated link ticks). → [One frame per tick of a 120 Hz display link](../reference/frame-rate.md#one-frame-per-tick-of-a-120-hz-display-link)
+- frames miss the screen while the GPU stays at its lowest clock (444 MHz on an M2 Max); more work (a higher render scale) only makes the frames slower. → [Present snapshots: the game's swapchain texture free at once](../reference/frame-rate.md#present-snapshots-the-games-swapchain-texture-free-at-once)
+- with snapshots on, Xenoblade Chronicles 3's 3D scene is one flat colour in a third to a half of the frames (black, grey, blue, orange, white; a new colour each time), while the HUD drawn over it is right. The more ring slots, the more often: 32 of 80 screen captures with 2 slots, 49 of 80 with 4, none with snapshots off (LNPANDY/NX2Apple#1117). → [Present snapshots: the game's swapchain texture free at once](../reference/frame-rate.md#present-snapshots-the-games-swapchain-texture-free-at-once)
+- none seen in a game. When the presented texture changes size or format while snapshots are on, a display command buffer still queued could sample the ring slot's old texture outside the residency set, which faults the queue (`kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorPageFault`, then every later command buffer ignored), as in A present snapshot's copy needs the same residency commit. → [A replaced snapshot ring texture stays resident until the slot's next use](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-replaced-snapshot-ring-texture-stays-resident-until-the-slots-next-use)
+- a game held at 30 fps on a 60 Hz display link shows each frame for two ticks; 60 fps doubles the CPU and GPU work, and on an iPhone the heat that comes with it (Ocarina of Time at Hyrule Castle reached thermal state 2 within 80 s at 60 fps). → [Frame generation between held presents](../reference/frame-rate.md#frame-generation-between-held-presents)
+- a steady game whose frame-rate counter wanders between 50 and 60 on the iPhone only; the `display link up to tick` lines show about as many empty ticks as frames dropped by merging: frames arrive two in one tick and none in the next. → [Vulkan acquire and vsync event follow the display link](../reference/frame-rate.md#vulkan-acquire-and-vsync-event-follow-the-display-link)
+- a `regress.py` run of an NVN game stays at 20 fps from the start (`frame N not reached`, a NEW-FAIL) in some runs only: `frame step: hold 1 → 2` and `2 → 3 vsyncs` in the first 15 presents, then `display link recovered: stalled 1017 ms` about once a second to the end, and no trial back to a shorter hold. → [A game window another process starts opens behind other windows](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-game-window-another-process-starts-opens-behind-other-windows)
+- asked for 60 Hz, the system fires the display link on every third refresh of a 165 Hz screen, 55 Hz, so games paced by it ran at 55 fps (27.5 at 30). → [Virtual 60 Hz ticks on screens that are not a multiple of 60](../reference/frame-rate.md#virtual-60-hz-ticks-on-screens-that-are-not-a-multiple-of-60)
+- virtual ticks keep the average, but a frame still stays up two or three refreshes in turn (13.9 / 20.8 ms at 144 Hz), and Apple's HUD showed the frame interval jumping between them. → [Fullscreen switches the screen to a rate the frame rate divides](../reference/frame-rate.md#fullscreen-switches-the-screen-to-a-rate-the-frame-rate-divides)
+- a Mac game in fullscreen holds 60 fps for 45 s to 3 minutes, then the same work takes about twice the GPU time (Tears of the Kingdom 10.5 → 19.7 ms) and the frame step falls to 30 fps. `powermetrics` shows the GPU at its lowest clock (338 MHz on an M4 Pro) while it is 72–98% busy, with thermal pressure nominal. Probes back to 60 fail, because the clock does not rise within a probe. `gamepolicyd` logs `Game mode enabled` and `Set DPS to 2`. → [macOS Game Mode holds the GPU at its lowest clock](../reference/frame-rate.md#macos-game-mode-holds-the-gpu-at-its-lowest-clock)
+- a Mac game started with `DM_FULLSCREEN=1` from an app that is itself in fullscreen (the launcher's console mode) came up as a window. → [A game opened from a fullscreen app stays a window](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-game-opened-from-a-fullscreen-app-stays-a-window)
+- on a ProMotion built-in screen, only in fullscreen, the frame step went 1 → 2 → 3 vsyncs on frames costing 0.1–0.6 vsyncs (`3 late frames; cost 0.32 vsyncs`) and the game presented 15 frames a second; a window, or the screen set to a fixed 60 Hz, did not do it. The `DM_NVN_TSV` rows show presents shown one 120 Hz refresh late (`@pres_at_ms` − `@link_target_ms` = 8.3 ms) with a skipped tick after each (`@link_gap_ms` 33 ms), and `@link_lead_ms` about 16 ms against about 50 ms in a window. → [Frame latency 2 for the display link in fullscreen](../reference/frame-rate.md#frame-latency-2-for-the-display-link-in-fullscreen)
+- in fullscreen on a MacBook Pro's ProMotion screen, a 60 fps game held 40 fps with its GPU at 10 ms a frame: frame times alternated 16.7 and 33.3 ms, and each `on-screen` line counted half its frames as held too long (`dropped 150` of 300, `discarded 0`). The same route in a window held 60.0 fps, and the title screen showed the same 40 fps in fullscreen. → [The 60 Hz link at 40 fps in fullscreen on a ProMotion screen](../reference/frame-rate.md#the-60-hz-link-at-40-fps-in-fullscreen-on-a-promotion-screen)
+- on a MacBook Air M2's fixed 60 Hz built-in display, the reporter measured about 50 ms from the game's present call to on-screen presentation with the display link, at both requested frame latencies 1 and 2, versus 29 ms with `DM_PRESENT_LINK=0`. After loading, all three runs held 60 fps with 0.3% of on-screen intervals outside 16.7 ± 2 ms and no frame-step changes. → [Fixed-rate display-link delay remains at latency 1](../reference/frame-rate.md#fixed-rate-display-link-delay-remains-at-latency-1)
+- at a 30 fps target, in fullscreen on a fixed 60 Hz screen, frames stay on screen 66.7 ms, often many in a row; in a window a late frame stays 50 ms. The `display link up to tick` lines count 300 ticks per 300 presents, but over 12-18 s instead of 10. → [The 30 Hz display link in fullscreen on a fixed-rate screen](../reference/frame-rate.md#the-30-hz-display-link-in-fullscreen-on-a-fixed-rate-screen)
+- autorelease work takes a measurable share of replay time, with temporary arena-buffer ownership traffic a few times per draw. → [Arena buffer ownership on allocation](../reference/frame-rate.md#arena-buffer-ownership-on-allocation)
+- a game runs at about 10 fps with little GPU work: the submit thread waits for a command allocator (`mtl_sub_begin` → `mtl_wait_at`), the GPU sits idle between encoders for hundreds of ms (`DM_NVN_GPUPROF`), the Metal 4 `SubmissionQueue` thread spends nearly all its time in `iokit_user_client_trap` (almost all system time), and the `memory up to frame` line shows a residency set of 10,000 objects or more. → [Dedicated c1 constants as slices of a bank](../reference/frame-rate.md#dedicated-c1-constants-as-slices-of-a-bank)
+- the submit thread waits for the GPU at the start of a submission (`mtl_sub_begin` → `mtl_wait_at`), many times a frame, in a game that makes many small submissions a frame; the `translation detail` line's `GPU waits so far` climbs with all 8 allocator pools in use. → [One command allocator for each of a frame's submissions](../reference/frame-rate.md#one-command-allocator-for-each-of-a-frames-submissions)
+- CPU-visible Metal memory (`footprint`'s `IOAccelerator (graphics)`) of several hundred MB in thousands of 32-48 KB regions, most of it the main submission's command allocators: `DM_NVN_ALLOCPROF=1`'s `command allocator usage` shows 8 of 36-80 MB each, a few MB each in light scenes. On some builds, frames with many render passes were slow while the GPU was not the limit. → [The alias flush on every encoder's end barrier](../reference/frame-rate.md#the-alias-flush-on-every-encoders-end-barrier)
+- the submit thread waits on the texture / sampler registry lock while a lower-priority registration thread is preempted holding it. → [Registry lock priority inheritance](../reference/frame-rate.md#registry-lock-priority-inheritance)
+- in a replay profile, `_platform_memcmp` is most of the time spent in serial lookups (`tex_mtl` through `g_by_serial`). → [Hash-table lookups of 8-byte keys](../reference/frame-rate.md#hash-table-lookups-of-8-byte-keys)
+- the GPU sits idle each frame while the host translates the submission (a game that does not submit frame N+1 until frame N's GPU work is done). → [Submissions handed to the GPU in segments](../reference/frame-rate.md#submissions-handed-to-the-gpu-in-segments)
+- one frame in 50 or so takes 25-40 ms in a scene where the frame normally fits, with the host on time: `@sync_ms` is high, `@sub_wall_ms` is not, and the frame before has a GPU span (`@gpu_end_ms - @gpu_beg_ms`) of 25-40 ms while the summed `@gpu_ms` has not moved. Runs fall into two states, and on the same code one can have 5 long frames and the next 60. A bad run also shows 186 % CPU against 133 %, and the previous frame still running when the next starts (p50 4 ms, p90 10 ms, against about 0 in a good run). → [The GPU idles with the next command buffer already committed](../reference/frame-rate.md#the-gpu-idles-with-the-next-command-buffer-already-committed)
+- the submit thread's CPU samples half in `__CFBasicHashFindBucket_Linear` under `CFSetContainsValue` (from `tex_mtl_` and `tex_by_id`), a few hundred thousand to over a million instructions of translation per draw, the submission growing with the game's CPU-written textures. → [The set of CPU-written textures hashed by serial](../reference/frame-rate.md#the-set-of-cpu-written-textures-hashed-by-serial)
+- several milliseconds of CPU translation and geometry preparation separate one frame's GPU end from the next frame's GPU start, even though the frame already contains complete geometry passes. → [Geometry boundaries submit prepared work early](../reference/frame-rate.md#geometry-boundaries-submit-prepared-work-early)
+- CPU submission spikes during texture updates, with the residency log showing tens of MB of new private staging allocations per frame. → [Reusing upload staging after a slot completes](../reference/frame-rate.md#reusing-upload-staging-after-a-slot-completes)
+- periodically rewritten textures cause CPU submission spikes even with no new staging allocations or shader compilation; a small cube-array image produces thousands of deswizzle dispatches and buffer-to-texture copies. → [Small array texture uploads without per-face staging](../reference/frame-rate.md#small-array-texture-uploads-without-per-face-staging)
+- the submit thread's CPU samples dominated by `mtl_addr` / `mtl_gpu_addr` (vertex buffers in `draw`, uniform buffers in `encode_args`). → [Address to memory pool lookups by binary search](../reference/frame-rate.md#address-to-memory-pool-lookups-by-binary-search)
+- a submit thread busy in `encode_args` with few blocks reused: the per-frame TSV's `@args_enc` close to `@draws`, though most draws keep their textures and samplers. → [Argument blocks encoded again when only a uniform buffer moved](../reference/frame-rate.md#argument-blocks-encoded-again-when-only-a-uniform-buffer-moved)
+- gameplay has low CPU clocks, large render submission wall time compared with thread CPU time, and default QoS (`0x15`) on the main guest thread while workers report user-interactive (`0x21`). → [Main guest thread keeps inherited default QoS](../reference/frame-rate.md#main-guest-thread-keeps-inherited-default-qos)
+- stalls of 30–60 ms about once a second, at 30 fps and at 60 (99th percentile 63–127 ms); the main thread waits 20–34 ms for the render thread once a second. → [Time-constraint computation declared below the thread's peaks](../reference/frame-rate.md#time-constraint-computation-declared-below-the-threads-peaks)
