+- a game with dynamic resolution stays at its full size while the GPU runs far over budget: `GPU p50` above the frame time target, frame times locked to 2 vsyncs, and the same pass sizes frame after frame in `DM_NVN_GPUPROF`. → [Dynamic resolution that never moves, and draw passes that wait for each other](../reference/frame-rate.md#dynamic-resolution-that-never-moves-and-draw-passes-that-wait-for-each-other)
+- a game with `draw_barriers game` gets no benefit from it: the 300-frame log line `draw-pass entry barriers full N / saved 0`, every draw pass behind a full barrier, and in a Metal System Trace many small idle gaps between consecutive render encoders of one command buffer (Ocarina of Time on an M1 Max: 141 a frame, 2.3 ms in all). → [Draw passes that wait for each other again while images share memory](../reference/frame-rate.md#draw-passes-that-wait-for-each-other-again-while-images-share-memory)
+- after a loading screen the game's resolution stays at its lowest (Ocarina of Time: 683x384) for minutes while it holds 60 fps with GPU time well under the frame, and climbs back only slowly or not at all. → [Dynamic resolution at its floor while the GPU has time to spare](../reference/frame-rate.md#dynamic-resolution-at-its-floor-while-the-gpu-has-time-to-spare)
+- many passes a frame on the same attachments, with the residency set line showing `passes ended to land them for a read` in the thousands and `refused …: earlier submission running` matching it. Ocarina of Time: 10,463 per 300 frames, each ending the pass over a uniform block no GPU write touched. → [A full table of running GPU writes ends passes for nothing](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-full-table-of-running-gpu-writes-ends-passes-for-nothing)
+- the submit (replay) thread spends a large share of its time in `ubc_wait`, under `pthread_mutex_lock` / `pthread_cond_signal`, while the `dm-ub-copy` thread waits for the same mutex far longer than it copies; translation cost jumps several-fold on one host build and not on another with the same code. Ocarina of Time on one build: 192 of the replay thread's 392 busy samples in `ubc_wait`, the copy thread 168 samples waiting for the mutex and 19 copying, 221-333k instructions a draw (9 runs) and 23-57 ms of submit CPU a frame, against 38.9k and 7.1 ms with `DM_NVN_UB_COPY_THREAD=0`. Builds that did not convoy still had 0.3-0.9 % of the replay thread's busy samples there. → [The uniform copy thread's hand-off convoys on its mutex](../reference/frame-rate.md#the-uniform-copy-threads-hand-off-convoys-on-its-mutex)
+- a game that reads textures through a pool (Shaders report which textures they read through a pool) renders far below its maximum dynamic resolution: Ocarina of Time's main pass at 763x429 with the GPU at 17 ms a frame. → [A host's own store in a fragment shader moves its depth test](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-hosts-own-store-in-a-fragment-shader-moves-its-depth-test)
+- passes that read textures through a pool (Shaders report which textures they read through a pool) cost far more GPU time than their texture reads explain, most on an iPad: Ocarina of Time's colour pass about 13 ms on an iPad Pro M5, 6 times the Mac's, while its prepass cost 1.3 times. → [Pool marks queue on one word when they test it with an atomic load](../reference/frame-rate.md#pool-marks-queue-on-one-word-when-they-test-it-with-an-atomic-load)
+- a busy scene runs far below what its GPU time allows, with the main thread busy for most of each second. The `pipelines up to frame` line shows archive lookups that found none in the thousands per 300 frames, at about 3 ms each, many draws with a hybrid pipeline, and `in flight 384`. Ocarina of Time's castle town market: 9,208 searches, 30.5 s in 300 frames, CPU translation 114 ms a frame. → [Archive searches repeated while the build table is full](../reference/frame-rate.md#archive-searches-repeated-while-the-build-table-is-full)
+- frames of 50–170 ms on entering an area, in every run again, although the prewarm lists and the pipeline archive hold everything the area draws. The long-frame line shows `new pipelines 0` or `1` with tens of milliseconds, and the `pipelines up to frame` line shows that time under `predictions`, with a hundred or more `archive lookups found` at about 1 ms each and as many `pulling pipelines made`, against one or two `draws with a hybrid pipeline`. Ocarina of Time's castle town: one prediction made 160 stand-ins in 104 ms, three predictions 244 in 191 ms. → [Pulling stand-ins made for predicted keys that are never drawn](../reference/frame-rate.md#pulling-stand-ins-made-for-predicted-keys-that-are-never-drawn)
+- frames of 30–150 ms as an area loads, with `new pipelines 0` and a long-frame line such as `texture uploads 31 first + 6 re-uploads, 235.0 MB / 23.7 ms`. With `DM_NVN_REUPLOADS=1` the same large texture is re-uploaded many times within a few frames, several times with the same `submission` number, each for a few written pages (`pages written since its upload: 7 of 2466`). → [A texture written while it is drawn read whole at every use](../reference/frame-rate.md#a-texture-written-while-it-is-drawn-read-whole-at-every-use)
