+## NVN objects
+
+- heap corruption; a stack smash where a function returned to `0x100000000`; a crash in SHA-256 on the code address 0x16300. → [Host records must fit the game's SDK object sizes](../reference/nvn-objects.md#host-records-must-fit-the-games-sdk-object-sizes)
+- out-of-range vertices, NaN, a black band and flicker; seen on the iPhone, while Mac runs of the same scene looked clean. → [Inlined SDK code writes into `NVNcommandBuffer` +0x10](../reference/nvn-objects.md#inlined-sdk-code-writes-into-nvncommandbuffer-0x10)
+- a running game with sound draws a black screen; every uniform buffer returns the same initialized address, with its allocation offset reported as its size. → [Guest ring allocators write `NVNbuffer` offset and size directly](../reference/nvn-objects.md#guest-ring-allocators-write-nvnbuffer-offset-and-size-directly)
+- a game registers the same few sampler states into its sampler pool again and again: Super Mario Odyssey made 3,368 to 5,737 `nvnSamplerPoolRegisterSampler` calls for 168 distinct states in two runs of about 110 s from the regress route's save, against 168 for 168 after the fix. Each re-registration rewrites a pool ID that recorded draws may still use. Its picture on that route (frames 4,500 to 6,300) was the same either way, apart from the hint video's corner, which also varies between runs of one host. → [Games hash a sampler's bytes from 8 on](../reference/nvn-objects.md#games-hash-a-samplers-bytes-from-8-on)
+- sprites broke apart in some runs only, depending on the allocation order; buffers overlapped. → [Sizes the hardware never reports as 0 (ZCull save area)](../reference/nvn-objects.md#sizes-the-hardware-never-reports-as-0-zcull-save-area)
+- a null write as soon as the main thread starts recording a command buffer (13 Sentinels: a constructor at `main+0x2fa9a0` runs on `this = 0` when the prologue enters its first battle). → [Heaps cut from a pool at alignment relative to its GPU address](../reference/nvn-objects.md#heaps-cut-from-a-pool-at-alignment-relative-to-its-gpu-address)
+- `[RED] memory pool … created at retry N`. → [`newBufferWithBytesNoCopy` retried under memory pressure](../reference/nvn-objects.md#newbufferwithbytesnocopy-retried-under-memory-pressure)
+- a buffer is created with a null pool right after a scene change. → [Virtual memory pools mapped onto one physical pool](../reference/nvn-objects.md#virtual-memory-pools-mapped-onto-one-physical-pool)
+- `[RED] MapVirtual: non-contiguous mapping (true sparse) not implemented`. → [Sparse virtual pools built from separate physical pools](../reference/nvn-objects.md#sparse-virtual-pools-built-from-separate-physical-pools)
+- `[RED] virtual pool …: more than 1024 separate mapped runs`, then a GPU hang minutes later: `[RED] the host has waited 10 s for GPU work`, an indirect dispatch of absurd size (`283343572 x 965150577 x 1006648320 groups`), and the game aborts once its 15 s `nvnSyncWait` expires. → [More than 1,024 mapped runs in a sparse pool](../reference/nvn-objects.md#more-than-1024-mapped-runs-in-a-sparse-pool)
+- in a game that maps and unmaps sparse pages while it runs: an indirect dispatch of absurd size, a GPU hang, then a crash in the game's own code reading a command list that holds another object's data. → [Pages changed under a buffer the GPU still uses](../reference/nvn-objects.md#pages-changed-under-a-buffer-the-gpu-still-uses)
+- `[RED] shader <key>: the buffer address the game pushed at c[24] + … is …, in no memory pool: bound to a dummy buffer`, once or twice in minutes, for an address inside a mapped run of a sparse pool; the draw or dispatch of that frame reads zeros. → [A sparse pool's runs replaced in two steps](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-sparse-pools-runs-replaced-in-two-steps)
+- `[RED] MapVirtual: non-contiguous mapping (true sparse) not implemented`, on small virtual pools; streamed textures black or missing (OCTOPATH TRAVELER's village ground and plants). → [Virtual pools of one texture each, mapped before and after the texture](../reference/nvn-objects.md#virtual-pools-of-one-texture-each-mapped-before-and-after-the-texture)
+- `[RED] NVN unimplemented function called: nvnTextureBuilderGetSparseTileLayout`, then an out-of-memory abort in Unreal's allocator (`Failed to allocate 1879113728 bytes from Standard Allocator`, on `DM_DBG=1`). → [The sparse tile layout of a texture](../reference/nvn-objects.md#the-sparse-tile-layout-of-a-texture)
+- `CRASH sig=10` in `memmove` from `pkg_upload` → `nvn_deswizzle`, at an address 32 GB into a sparse virtual pool; textures with unaliased tails came out magenta. → [Textures read through a 4 KB page table of a sparse pool](../reference/nvn-objects.md#textures-read-through-a-4-kb-page-table-of-a-sparse-pool)
+- `kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorOutOfMemory` on every command buffer from the first frames, no frame presented, "GPU allocated" near the Mac's memory; `large texture … in a sparse virtual pool` lines name textures of gigabytes. → [Large sparse textures as Metal placement sparse textures](../reference/nvn-objects.md#large-sparse-textures-as-metal-placement-sparse-textures)
+- the placement heaps (`sparse tiles: placement heap N`) keep growing through a session, to several times the memory the game maps behind its sparse textures. On a 16 GB M1 Pro, Mario Kart World's race reached 11 heaps (2.75 GB) within three minutes, wired memory 8.7 GB, and the system swapped: frames went from 25 to 50 ms. A GPU write through one place of a page is also not seen at another. → [Physical pages mapped at several places of a placement sparse texture](../reference/nvn-objects.md#physical-pages-mapped-at-several-places-of-a-placement-sparse-texture)
+- frames of 100 ms and more every few frames on a Mac short of memory, with no failing command buffer; a Metal System Trace shows the driver wiring and unwiring all the time (3.2 s of a 6 s trace on the game below), and the `restop` line of `DM_CTL` shows gigabytes of shared buffers. → [Large pools wrapped in chunks](../reference/nvn-objects.md#large-pools-wrapped-in-chunks)
+- what the GPU copies, fills or renders into a pool never shows, with no error and no red line: garbled text where the game copies its font textures inside one pool. `DM_NVN_POOL_CHUNKS=0` clears it when the buffer is a chunk. → [GPU writes dropped by a pool buffer made over write-protected pages](../reference/nvn-objects.md#gpu-writes-dropped-by-a-pool-buffer-made-over-write-protected-pages)
+- GPU memory grows with every scene change (Engage's idle title demo reached 5.4 GB of GPU memory and 3.6 GB resident in 850 s). → [Finalized pools kept alive by residency and the sharing table](../reference/nvn-objects.md#finalized-pools-kept-alive-by-residency-and-the-sharing-table)
+- the memory line's `freed but alive` count only grows, while no host table holds those buffers; "GPU allocated" is far above resident memory (Ghost Trick: Phantom Detective, 125 to 146 buffers and 6.6 to 8.7 GB of GPU allocations against 2.2 GB resident). → [Finalized pool buffers kept by a guest thread's autorelease pool](../reference/nvn-objects.md#finalized-pool-buffers-kept-by-a-guest-threads-autorelease-pool)
+- GPU and resident memory keep growing during texture streaming while pool, residency and serial-object counts stay nearly flat, with no freed pool buffers alive. → [Finalized textures kept by registered descriptor views](../reference/nvn-objects.md#finalized-textures-kept-by-registered-descriptor-views)
+- command buffers fail with `kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorOutOfMemory` at a scene change, after GPU memory jumps past Metal's recommended working set (16 GB allocated against 12.1 GB on a 16 GB Mac); the `pool for handles traced to no slot` lines show texture pool tables of tens of thousands of IDs rebuilt around it. → [Finalized textures made again by pool tables](../reference/nvn-objects.md#finalized-textures-made-again-by-pool-tables)
+- the submit thread spends milliseconds a frame in `pool_gpu` → `tex_by_id` → `tex_mtl` / `tex_get` (a `sample` of the process), and the `[nvn-mtl] pool for handles traced to no slot … built in X ms` line shows a rebuild of the texture pool table every frame at about 2 ms. Fire Emblem Fortune's Weave's battle map: 1.9 ms a build, three builds a frame, about 14 % of the main thread. → [Texture ID views revalidated only for the serials that changed](../reference/nvn-objects.md#texture-id-views-revalidated-only-for-the-serials-that-changed)
+- the `[nvn-mtl] pool for handles traced to no slot` line counts thousands of builds while the game streams, and the submit thread spends milliseconds a frame walking the texture pool table again. Ocarina of Time, a seven-minute walk through Kokiri Forest: about 61,000 builds of its 33,025-entry table, 53 s in all. → [Pool tables patched instead of built whole](../reference/nvn-objects.md#pool-tables-patched-instead-of-built-whole)
+- a pool table keeps naming an sRGB / linear twin's view after the texture it is a view of left the sharing table and, 17 submissions later, the residency set: a texture ID with no residency behind it, which gives GPU address faults in the shaders that sample it (as in issue #1326), and a view that no longer shares its storage's bytes with anything. Seen in `t_pool_notes` only, in no game. → [A view's entry did not hold its parent](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-views-entry-did-not-hold-its-parent)
+- the serial table grows by about 10 entries a frame (276,000 after 25,000 frames). → [Dead texture serials kept in a ghost table](../reference/nvn-objects.md#dead-texture-serials-kept-in-a-ghost-table)
+- `nvn_replay` aborts on a game's capture with `-[NvProg width]: unrecognized selector` (from `rt_dims`) or `-[NvProg mipmapLevelCount]`: a render target resolves to a program. → [A replay's own objects take serials above a capture's](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-replays-own-objects-take-serials-above-a-captures)
+- `DM_NVN_CAPTURE` reports `a record names … (0 bytes), outside every pool … texture registry entry …` for some texture IDs, with addresses that look like data; one capture stopped with `a sparse virtual pool (step 1 does not capture page tables): the capture is incomplete`, another with `straddles a region`. → [A captured texture ID whose memory pool was finalized](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-captured-texture-id-whose-memory-pool-was-finalized)
+- `CRASH sig=11 … pc=nvn_sparse_protect+0x40`, an address that looks like data, `x8` 8 below it, after a long session; the backtrace `tex_by_id` ← `tex_mtl` ← `pkg_upload`. The pool need not be sparse. → [A texture read after its memory pool object was freed](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-texture-read-after-its-memory-pool-object-was-freed)
+- `regress.py capture` stops with `frame N was not captured within … s` although the game reached the frame before it; a capture takes 5 to 20 minutes where an earlier one of the same frame took 2 to 3, and a sample of the host shows the submitting thread in `lzfseEncodeBase` under `nvn_cap_submit`. → [A frame capture runs under the standard thread policy](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-frame-capture-runs-under-the-standard-thread-policy)
+- `nvn_replay` stops with `program #N (a key …) stage S: the translated shader's bindings or inputs differ from the ones the records were captured against`, and `regress.py replay` reports every game's capture as one the candidate cannot replay, while the shaders' reflection (`<key>_b.json`) is the same in both chains apart from the chain field. → [A capture refused after a change to the reflection structs](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-capture-refused-after-a-change-to-the-reflection-structs)
+- `texture object no longer valid`, followed by `CRASH sig=10` in `nvn_sparse_pool` through `sparse_gpu_wrote` and `attach`. In Rhythm Heaven Groove's frog stage, the supposed texture contained a texture handle followed by an `OP_DRAW_IDX` record. → [Bound render targets outlive command recordings](../reference/nvn-objects.md#bound-render-targets-outlive-command-recordings)
+- `[RED] NVN unimplemented function called: nvnCommandBufferGetControlMemoryFree` (or `…GetControlMemorySize`) once, after the game gives a command buffer control memory. → [Control memory capacity queries](../reference/nvn-objects.md#control-memory-capacity-queries)
+- `[RED] NVN unimplemented function called: nvnQueueBuilderGetCommandMemorySize` (or `…GetComputeMemorySize`, `…GetControlMemorySize`, `…GetRayTracingMemorySize`, `nvnQueueBuilderSetRayTracingMemorySize`), once each, as the game makes its queue. → [Queue builder size getters](../reference/nvn-objects.md#queue-builder-size-getters)
+- `[RED] NVN unimplemented function called: nvnCommandBufferSetFloatBlendClamp`; now `[RED] nvnCommandBufferSetFloatBlendClamp 1 (a float blend result that overflows becomes the largest finite value): Metal stores infinity`, once. → [Float blend clamp (`nvnCommandBufferSetFloatBlendClamp`)](../reference/nvn-objects.md#float-blend-clamp-nvncommandbuffersetfloatblendclamp)
+- `FATAL NVN command memory: the game's pool is empty…`. → [Host-lent command memory segments](../reference/nvn-objects.md#host-lent-command-memory-segments)
+- frames stop after about 15 s; a game thread spins in its command-memory callback (a ring allocator at `main+0x9cc120` waiting for fences that have all signalled). → [What `GetCommandMemoryUsed` counts](../reference/nvn-objects.md#what-getcommandmemoryused-counts)
+- `CRASH sig=10` in `mtl_exec` after minutes of play, the fault address equal to `x19` and shaped like `0x1000000003`. → [Prerecorded lists in slots of their measured size](../reference/nvn-objects.md#prerecorded-lists-in-slots-of-their-measured-size)
+- a model load runs out of its resource heap: `[RED] BufferInitialize: pool has no storage (pool 0x0 …)`, then `CRASH sig=11` in `h_nvnMemoryPoolGetFlags` (Model heaps sized by the host's command record sizes). → [A recording's end and links are in control memory](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-recordings-end-and-links-are-in-control-memory)
+- a jump to pc=0 on a worker thread, with the backtrace `0x0` ← `cmd_alloc_keep` ← `h_nvnCommandBufferCopyBufferToTexture` and `lr` in the game's `main`, just after the instruction that called the game's command memory callback. → [Copy strides are command buffer state](../reference/nvn-objects.md#copy-strides-are-command-buffer-state)
+- `CRASH sig=11 addr=0x50` on a game thread, at `pc=main+0x385201c` in Super Smash Bros. Ultimate (`ldr x0, [x8, #0x10]` with `x8 = 0x40`), as a battle starts. Rare: it crashed on 1 of 2 battle starts in one run on a Mac mini and on none of 11 in another. → [`GetCommandMemorySize` read while another thread records](../reference/nvn-objects.md#getcommandmemorysize-read-while-another-thread-records)
+- the title showed one stretched 64×64 icon over the 3D scene, a new game a white screen; post-process passes with stale texture handles; `[RED] bind record … overwritten after recording`. → [Recorded commands patched in place by the game (`OP_BIND_TEX1`)](../reference/nvn-objects.md#recorded-commands-patched-in-place-by-the-game-op_bind_tex1)
+- intermittent scene-wide lighting flashes and a persistent pale outline around a character. Extra GPU readbacks or synchronous screenshots can make the flashes disappear temporarily. Separately: twice the render passes and attachment loads a frame, with a correct picture, in a game that signals events inside its passes. → [GPU event waits order work across queues](../reference/nvn-objects.md#gpu-event-waits-order-work-across-queues)
+- choosing X-2 after the collection launcher crashes in `keys_hash`, reading control address `0x8000000000`; the program object now contains command-buffer data. → [Bindings belong to each queue](../reference/nvn-objects.md#bindings-belong-to-each-queue)
+- about 23 s after launch, when the title screen hands over to the opening demo, a crash in `keys_hash` (`prog_key` ← `prog_get` ← `bind_stages` ← `prog_resolve` ← `mtl_queue_activate`), reading a control address made of float data; the program object now holds other data. → [Finalized programs leave every queue's bindings](../reference/nvn-objects.md#finalized-programs-leave-every-queues-bindings)
+- `nvnCommandBufferFenceSync` unimplemented, every `SyncWait` timed out at once. → [Fence syncs recorded in command buffers](../reference/nvn-objects.md#fence-syncs-recorded-in-command-buffers)
+- `FATAL submit re-entered` in the first frames. → [Submissions from two queues on two threads](../reference/nvn-objects.md#submissions-from-two-queues-on-two-threads)
+- going to the background (iOS `sceneDidEnterBackground`, minimizing the Mac window) while the "Preparing shaders" or "Preparing pipelines" screen is up blocks the main thread until the step ends: minutes on a first start, long enough for iOS to end the app. The log's `[nvn] graphics suspended` line comes only after the `pipeline prepare:` line. → [Going to the background during the prepare step](../reference/nvn-objects.md#going-to-the-background-during-the-prepare-step)
+- frames stop, no crash, the main thread sits in `nvnSyncWait(sync, -1)`, and the watchdog's wait-state line shows the main fence one short (`main fence submitted N / completed N-1`). → [A GPU command buffer that never completes](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-gpu-command-buffer-that-never-completes)
+- `game called nn::diag abort` right after a screen change (PRAGMATA: the Initial Settings menu's Next), with nothing slow before it. → [A sync never fenced since its Initialize is signalled](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-sync-never-fenced-since-its-initialize-is-signalled)
+- the frame's blocking SyncWait on its newest fence wakes about 0.26–0.29 ms after the GPU end (M2 Max; the wait ledger, `DM_NVN_WAITS=1`), on the critical path of every frame. → [The game's SyncWait wakes on the GPU's own signal of its fence](../reference/nvn-objects.md#the-games-syncwait-wakes-on-the-gpus-own-signal-of-its-fence)
+- loading screens many times slower on the iPhone than on the Mac (Three Houses' first loading screen 78 s on the iPhone, 5 s on the Mac). → [`nvnQueueFinish` waits for the GPU, not the display](../reference/nvn-objects.md#nvnqueuefinish-waits-for-the-gpu-not-the-display)
+- CPU texture uploads spend time recalculating block-linear addresses and calling variable-length `memcpy` for every 16-byte segment. → [CPU deswizzle copies use slice and row offsets](../reference/nvn-objects.md#cpu-deswizzle-copies-use-slice-and-row-offsets)
+- a Switch 2 game's textures from its files keep their shapes but break up along every edge: misplaced 4×4 blocks (16-byte pieces) in a regular weave, invisible in flat areas. Normal maps make lit surfaces look woven; opacity masks punch holes. → [The Switch 2's order inside a GOB](../reference/nvn-objects.md#the-switch-2s-order-inside-a-gob)
+- driver comparisons report the wrong block dimensions for packaged 2D / 3D textures, or too little storage for linear block-compressed textures (64x64 BC1 with stride 128: 2048 bytes instead of 8192). → [Packaged texture block dimensions and linear storage rows](../reference/nvn-objects.md#packaged-texture-block-dimensions-and-linear-storage-rows)
+- huge stretched terrain walls and ceilings, flickering, while characters and the HUD are right: `WriteTexels` into a texture overwrote the mesh data stored right after it. → [Minimal texture layouts](../reference/nvn-objects.md#minimal-texture-layouts)
+- menus and dialogue boxes draw but contain no text, while images and pictogram digits are fine; the sky is black (CPU-written linear colour ramps). → [Linear textures in CPU-visible pools](../reference/nvn-objects.md#linear-textures-in-cpu-visible-pools)
+- a trap during graphics initialization in `SetupSurfaceInfoMemoryInside`, asserting `ctx.memorySize <= localmemsetupinfo.memorySize`. → [Linear render-target storage without the LINEAR flag](../reference/nvn-objects.md#linear-render-target-storage-without-the-linear-flag)
+- on an iPhone only, a scene's backgrounds are solid magenta and magenta frames flash, while the Mac is right. The log has `[RED] linear texture offset … / row pitch 32 not aligned to 64 bytes; created as a private texture (CPU writes invisible)`, then `[RED] deswizzle failed format 0x25 target 1`. Or movie backgrounds are grey and flicker, or show green and magenta blocks, after `linear texture ser=… 736x480 format 0x1 flags 0x14: offset … / row pitch 736 not aligned to 64 bytes` and `[RED] linear texture 8x8 format 0x9a flags 0x14: rows of 32 bytes no view can take, a private texture the CPU's writes do not reach`. → [Linear textures a device cannot view on their pool's buffer](../reference/nvn-objects.md#linear-textures-a-device-cannot-view-on-their-pools-buffer)
+- video frames, or a game's built-in default textures, are all zero. → [Textures written with `nvnTextureWriteTexels`](../reference/nvn-objects.md#textures-written-with-nvntexturewritetexels)
+- after a few minutes of play the main thread spends whole frames in `WriteTexels` (Metroid Dread's frames stopped at about 175 s). → [`WriteTexels` regions written in place by block-linear address](../reference/nvn-objects.md#writetexels-regions-written-in-place-by-block-linear-address)
+- a movie in native-texture mode draws black (or a texture the game fills with `WriteTexels` stays empty), and the texture is `LINEAR` or `LINEAR_RENDER_TARGET`. When only part of the texture is written, the log has `[RED] WriteTexels deswizzle failed`. A write of the whole texture logged nothing. → [`WriteTexels` into a linear texture dropped the write](../reference/nvn-objects.md#writetexels-into-a-linear-texture-dropped-the-write)
+- black screen with every geometry right (`DM_NVN_HILITE` shows each sprite and glyph in place); the uniform buffer right (a `CBDUMP`), so every texture read 0. → [A pool's storage at creation is its textures' content](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-pools-storage-at-creation-is-its-textures-content)
+- a game draws its UI and text but every image is black; `packaged textures: first uploads 0` while `TextureInitialize` counts the textures the game created (SD GUNDAM G GENERATION CROSS RAYS: 74, of which the 10 BC3 ones hold data and none is uploaded). After the fill works, one overlay still comes up with its text over nothing: the panel's background is missing and the text of the next block sits on top of it. → [`COMPRESSIBLE` in a pool the CPU writes is only a hint](../reference/nvn-objects.md#compressible-in-a-pool-the-cpu-writes-is-only-a-hint)
+- a texture the game filled with `memcpy` and flushed reads as zeros (BotW's sky brownish grey, the distant haze brown). → [First-use fill of storage the game flushed](../reference/nvn-objects.md#first-use-fill-of-storage-the-game-flushed)
+- magenta (ASTC's error colour) where UI images belong: a title logo and menu panels, while the 3D scene and the text draw; `DM_NVN_ZBLOCK=1` reports nothing (the textures are not uploaded on the CPU at all); `DM_NVN_TEXINFO=WxH` shows storage holding data at first use (`non-zero …`) in a pool with flags 0x22 (`CPU_UNCACHED | GPU_CACHED`). → [Storage of a CPU-uncached pool at first use](../reference/nvn-objects.md#storage-of-a-cpu-uncached-pool-at-first-use)
+- a picture that should change with a selection is black, or shows the previous item's picture while the item's name and description are right. `DM_NVN_TEXINFO=WxH` shows `swizzled=0` at first use with storage holding data, no packaged layout, in a `CPU_CACHED` or `CPU_UNCACHED` pool, and `DM_NVN_DIRTYCHECK=1` reports no missed write. → [Block-compressed storage the CPU wrote before first use](../reference/nvn-objects.md#block-compressed-storage-the-cpu-wrote-before-first-use)
+- a movie plays black (Age of Calamity: its intro movie under the "+ Skip" prompt). → [Mapped-range flushes taken as uploads (`tex_flush_upload`)](../reference/nvn-objects.md#mapped-range-flushes-taken-as-uploads-tex_flush_upload)
+- open-world ground is one flat colour on a flat plane with blocky seams, or water and sand are missing, while course meshes, rocks and the kart look right. `DM_NVN_TEXINFO=WxH` shows uncompressed textures with `swizzled=0`, no packaged layout and storage holding data in a `CPU_CACHED` pool. → [Uncompressed storage the CPU wrote, in a pool the GPU has not written](../reference/nvn-objects.md#uncompressed-storage-the-cpu-wrote-in-a-pool-the-gpu-has-not-written)
+- after some walking, the ground opens in holes (black, or the water and sky below showing through) and the hole spreads to areas loaded later, including ones that were fine before; saving and loading the game makes it right again for a while. A diagnostic line at the GPU texture's creation shows uncompressed terrain textures in a `CPU_CACHED | GPU_CACHED` pool (flags 0x24) whose storage holds data, not flushed, made as GPU textures of zeros. → [Uncompressed storage beside storage the GPU writes](../reference/nvn-objects.md#uncompressed-storage-beside-storage-the-gpu-writes)
+- after some walking, a whole terrain tile is missing (the ground vanishes, or the tile sits flat and low) or shows another surface with a hard square edge (a square of grass across a cobbled path, a grass patch on a mountain trail); a reload makes it right for a while. The host's copy of the tile's 260x260 R32F height map or RGBA8 hole map is all zeros while the game's memory holds data (`DM_NVN_TEXINFO=260x260` shows storage non-zero at first use; a `texraw` dump of the host texture is zero). → [A span's sliver inside a texture](../reference/nvn-objects.md#a-spans-sliver-inside-a-texture)
+- magenta surfaces (Metal decodes an all-zero ASTC block as the error colour (1, 0, 1)); a zero BC block decodes black, which looks like missing light; black screen while the UI (or a dialog box) draws in the right places under `HILITE` or in the pass dump; `packaged textures: first uploads 0`. → [CPU writes with no flush, caught by page protection (`tex_dirty_upload`)](../reference/nvn-objects.md#cpu-writes-with-no-flush-caught-by-page-protection-tex_dirty_upload)
+- a texture the game builds from CPU-filled textures read back with `nvnTextureReadTexels` comes out zero. → [`ReadTexels` from guest memory and textures written after first use](../reference/nvn-objects.md#readtexels-from-guest-memory-and-textures-written-after-first-use)
+- with `tex_dirty_upload`, a texture the CPU fills again after its first use keeps its old content: Ocarina of Time's ground opened flat blue holes with grass and rocks floating over them (Kokiri Forest, the village floor, the Deku Tree) after an hour of play, as terrain tile slots were reused; no red line, and the frame's `texture uploads … re-uploads` stays at 0 while the pool's pages change. → [Textures made for GPU use kept from their CPU rewrites](../reference/nvn-objects.md#textures-made-for-gpu-use-kept-from-their-cpu-rewrites)
+- `[RED] upload batches belong to the submit thread only: up_begin called on thread '…'`, then an abort, with `pkg_upload` ← `tex_mtl` ← `mtl_read_texels` on the stack; or `CRASH sig=10` in `nvn_deswizzle` ← `mtl_read_texels`, at an address in a sparse virtual pool's reserved range. → [`ReadTexels` on a texture of a sparse pool](../reference/nvn-objects.md#readtexels-on-a-texture-of-a-sparse-pool)
+- a streamed texture's small mip levels hold another texture or zeros while its large levels are right (`texraw <serial>.<level>`), so surfaces sampled with a mip bias or at a distance show bright speckles, dark blotches and sharp detail where the Switch shows soft texture (Yoshi's felt), from the moment finer levels stream in (the opening going dark). → [GPU copies into textures uploaded from their storage](../reference/nvn-objects.md#gpu-copies-into-textures-uploaded-from-their-storage)
