+- `pso_offline.py` holds about 1.8 GB while `metal-tt` builds an archive of 140,036 bases, and the system's memory view shows the build far larger than that (180.5 GB) before `metal-tt` fails or is stopped. → [Python memory during offline pipeline archive preparation](../reference/shaders.md#python-memory-during-offline-pipeline-archive-preparation)
+- the offline archive stops with only `applegpu-nt command failed`, sometimes after `Max Unstruct ID` lines (a note, not the failure); the macOS kernel log reports `no paging space action` and kills `air-nt` as the largest compressed process. → [Bounded groups for the offline pipeline compiler](../reference/shaders.md#bounded-groups-for-the-offline-pipeline-compiler)
+- `[RED] pso_offline: metal-tt failed for applegpu_… (exit 1): LLVM ERROR: Failed to encode.`, and the NSP script's `[RED] pipeline archive: pso_offline.py failed …; the app is packed without one and builds its pipeline bases on its first start`. On a run from the build folder: `[RED] pipeline prebuild: the base of … failed: Compilation failed due to an interrupted connection: XPC_ERROR_CONNECTION_INTERRUPTED`, a `MTLCompilerService` crash report whose stack ends in a fatal error under `llvm::MCObjectStreamer::emitInstruction` (`Error message SHA256: 01fbda05…`, the hash of `Failed to encode.`), and the compiles in flight when the service ends failing with it. → [A base the GPU compiler cannot encode cost the whole offline archive](../reference/shaders.md#a-base-the-gpu-compiler-cannot-encode-cost-the-whole-offline-archive)
+- `--platform mac-app` or the iPhone build stops at its pipeline archive stage, after every earlier stage has finished: `[RED] pso_offline: metal-tt failed for applegpu_<gpu> (exit 1)`, then `pso_offline.py exit code 1`. → [A failed pipeline archive stops the whole build](../reference/shaders.md#a-failed-pipeline-archive-stops-the-whole-build)
+- `[RED] pso_offline: metal-tt failed for applegpu_<gpu> (exit 1)` with only `applegpu-nt command failed` in `metal-tt.<arch>.log`. → [Offline pipeline archive on a full disk](../reference/shaders.md#offline-pipeline-archive-on-a-full-disk)
+- a game without a recorded prewarm list (a provisional profile) stalls at its first dispatches while Metal compiles their compute pipelines. The Mac app's pipeline archive held render bases only (`archive.tsv`: `bases`), whatever the shader map listed. → [Compute pipelines compiled at first use](../reference/shaders.md#compute-pipelines-compiled-at-first-use)
+- a compute pipeline the archive bundled with the app does not hold (no bundle, as in a headless boot or a build without `pso_offline.py`; a shader outside the shader map; an OS update the bundle's binaries do not survive) was compiled again at its first dispatch of every start the system's shader cache had dropped it, and the device's own pipeline archive (`pso_archive`) never held one: `compute pipelines so far: 0 from the archive bundled with the app, N built` start after start. → [Compute pipelines outside the bundled archive built at every start](../reference/shaders.md#compute-pipelines-outside-the-bundled-archive-built-at-every-start)
+- the first sight of a scene drawn with task and mesh shaders freezes for seconds on a cold Metal cache, on every start once the system's shader cache has dropped the pipelines, although the prewarm finished: `task / mesh pipeline: task … mesh … fragment …, N ms required at frame F`. Before ea0c98df the draw was left out instead, and the foliage stayed missing or white until the pipeline was ready. → [Native mesh pipelines compiled at their first draw](../reference/shaders.md#native-mesh-pipelines-compiled-at-their-first-draw)
+- a frame of 200 ms or more the first time an effect appears, although the bundled pipeline archive was trusted and the prewarm finished. With `DM_PSO_WAITLOG=1` the frame shows waits on render pipelines with no pulling entry, 34-74 ms each, for vertex shaders that have no stage inputs. → [Vertex shaders with layer entry points left out of the prebuild](../reference/shaders.md#vertex-shaders-with-layer-entry-points-left-out-of-the-prebuild)
+- `[RED] pipeline prewarm: seed list does not exist: …/pso_manifest.tsv` at every start, no `pipeline prewarm list …/pso_user.tsv` line, and the same compile stalls every session in places already visited, while `pso_user.tsv` keeps growing; the pipeline archive gains files every start and is never compacted (Donkey Kong Bananza: 88 files, 7 s to open them). → [The device's prewarm list unread without a seed list](../reference/shaders.md#the-devices-prewarm-list-unread-without-a-seed-list)
+- the first frame that draws with new programs loads their shader libraries on the submit thread (`[longframe] ... shader libraries N, X ms`): tens of milliseconds with the files cached, seconds when the drive is slow. → [Shader libraries loaded when the program is made](../reference/shaders.md#shader-libraries-loaded-when-the-program-is-made)
+- at boot, `CRASH sig=5 … pc=_dispatch_once_wait.cold.1` right after the `pipeline bases so far` line, with `bt: pso_prebuild __shmap_load_block_invoke … mtl_prog_prefetch h_nvnProgramSetShaders`. → [The prepare step inside the shader map's once](../reference/shaders.md#the-prepare-step-inside-the-shader-maps-once)
+- after an IPA packed without `_bp.metallib` ran once with `DM_PSO_MTL4=1`, an app with those libraries still stalls on new pipelines: `pipelines up to frame` lines show `draws with a hybrid pipeline 0` and `draws that waited: pulling base not made yet`, and the log has no `pipeline prebuild:` line. The run before it logged `[RED] pulling library of … not usable` and `pipeline prebuild done: … 19838 left to the draws`. → [A prebuild stamp written without the pulling libraries](../reference/shaders.md#a-prebuild-stamp-written-without-the-pulling-libraries)
+- the user cache folder (`getconf DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR`, then `com.apple.gpuarchiver/`) fills with `gpuarchiver-*` folders of 0.3–5 MB each, one per pipeline archive flush (456 folders, 2.5 GB after two days). Later folders are larger, up to about 127 MB (an M4 Pro, 2026-10-29: 19 GB in 423 folders after a day of Mac runs and regression checks); why they grew is **open**. A later compile can fail with `cannot open '…/gpuarchiver-…/PersistentState': No such file or directory`. → [Metal's build folders left by pipeline archive flushes](../reference/shaders.md#metals-build-folders-left-by-pipeline-archive-flushes)
+- a previously working game exits with 139 on every start after pipeline preparation; `dispatch_retain(NULL)` is called by `-[_MTLBinaryArchive materializeAirNTFromOnlineForSlice:entryList:]` under `airntSerializeToURL` and `arch_flush` (issue #342). → [A compaction flush that crashes, then crashes every start](../reference/shaders.md#a-compaction-flush-that-crashes-then-crashes-every-start)
+- identical render pipelines compile repeatedly under different keys, lengthening startup prewarm and adding pipeline misses during play. → [Pipeline keys omit unused colour state](../reference/shaders.md#pipeline-keys-omit-unused-colour-state)
+- a vertex shader whose pairs changed was restored unpadded from `cas/`. → [Shader cache key covering the pairs](../reference/shaders.md#shader-cache-key-covering-the-pairs)
+- an edit to a shader build helper reaches no library: `shader cache: hit` for every shader, and the old MSL is filed under the new chain version. An edit to `dmsh_meshstage.py` did not change the chain version either. → [Shader cache key missing helper modules](../reference/shaders.md#shader-cache-key-missing-helper-modules)
+- a full build reports successful shaders, but `metal-tt` rejects its pipeline archive with fragment inputs not written by the vertex shader. Fifteen cached vertices lacked default outputs in DRAGON QUEST I & II. → [Partial vertex builds missing paired pixel sources](../reference/shaders.md#partial-vertex-builds-missing-paired-pixel-sources)
+- a repeated iOS-only shader build crashes with `FileNotFoundError` for `<key>_b.json` in the Mac output directory; requesting only Mac libraries with `IOSOUT` set still compiles iOS side libraries. → [Target selection keeps the shader analysis files in the cache](../reference/shaders.md#target-selection-keeps-the-shader-analysis-files-in-the-cache)
+- after `scripts/build_ipa_from_nsp.py <NSP folder> --platform mac`, a plain (iOS) run of the script on the same folder succeeds, but the Mac run command the first build printed then logs hundreds of `[RED] failed to load shader <key>_b: cannot open …/shader/cache/b-<chain>/<key>_b.metallib` lines, until `--platform mac` is run again. → [An iOS build after a Mac-only build kept no Mac libraries](../reference/shaders.md#an-ios-build-after-a-mac-only-build-kept-no-mac-libraries)
+- the build folder of a game (`<id>_build/shader/`) gains about 2 GB for Breath of the Wild with every upstream update that changes the shader chain: after two updates `shader/cache` held `b-157e1b96c8da` (2.1 GB) and `b-a78f966a7b38` (2.3 GB) besides the current one, and the next build added a third. → [Old shader library folders grew a game's build folder with each chain change](../reference/shaders.md#old-shader-library-folders-grew-a-games-build-folder-with-each-chain-change)
+- `<id>_build/shader/cache/cas/shader/` holds several entries for each shader, and grows by a full set with every upstream update that changes the shader chain. Tears of the Kingdom (seedds/nxnative#1033): 172,014 entries after six builds for 28,669 shaders, 11.85 GB of `.metal` and `.json` that no build read; the test Mac's copy held 934,955, of which the prune (dry run) found 906,286 unused: 70.2 GB of sources and reflection, scanned in 18 minutes on an external SSD. → [Old per-shader cache entries grew a game's build folder with each chain change](../reference/shaders.md#old-per-shader-cache-entries-grew-a-games-build-folder-with-each-chain-change)
+- the NSP script's Mac or iOS shader step for a game with very large shader packages runs at one or two shaders a second whatever `J` is. Its Python process sits at one full core, mostly system time, while few `metal` compiles run. Pokémon Pokopia (4.4 GB of packages, 131,426 shaders): players reported ETAs of 20 to 200 hours (seedds/nxnative#1036). → [A shader build that keeps its packages in memory runs one worker at a time](../reference/shaders.md#a-shader-build-that-keeps-its-packages-in-memory-runs-one-worker-at-a-time)
+- the Mac or iOS shader step of a game with a large map still takes many hours with every worker busy, and a `metal -c` of a translated shader takes about 0.7 s of CPU, nearly all in the front end. Optimising adds about 0.1 s, and an empty shader takes 0.025 s. Pokémon Pokopia's 131,426 shaders: about 9 hours on an M4 Pro (seedds/nxnative#1036). → [Each Metal compile instantiates Metal's texture classes again](../reference/shaders.md#each-metal-compile-instantiates-metals-texture-classes-again)
+- the shader step of a game with large translated sources has fewer Metal compiles running than `J` asks for, and `dmsh_build.py`'s own process stays busy on one core between them. Pokémon Pokopia's sources average 220 KB, and the builder spent about 0.12 s of Python on each shader, all but its system calls under the GIL, which alone would have come to about 4.2 hours of its full build. → [Rewrites that try every character of a large source](../reference/shaders.md#rewrites-that-try-every-character-of-a-large-source)
+- a cold shader step leaves about half the cores idle whatever `J` is, and `dmsh_build.py`'s own process stays at one full core from start to end. Ocarina of Time's whole Mac map, 42,848 shaders: 3,506 s on an M3 Max with 16 cores (`J=16`, the build at 104% CPU, 4 to 5 cores busy in all), 3,002 s on an M5 Max with 18 cores. → [A shader build's Python finishes one shader at a time](../reference/shaders.md#a-shader-builds-python-finishes-one-shader-at-a-time)
+- the Mac shader step stops a few seconds after it starts, every time, with `concurrent.futures.process.BrokenProcessPool` (since 8b55ebb3; the `[RED] a shader build worker process stopped` line once the build prints it), and `DMSH_PROCS=0` builds. The worker's crash report: `SIGSEGV` in `_os_log_preferences_refresh` ← `os_signpost_enabled` ← `os_signpost_id_make_with_pointer` ← `openDatabase` (`libsqlite3.dylib`) ← Python's `sqlite3`. Only with a Python whose `_sqlite3` module links Apple's `/usr/lib/libsqlite3.dylib` (`otool -L` on `_sqlite3.__file__`): Xcode's, and by the reports some Python 3.12+ builds; Homebrew's Python has its own SQLite and builds. → [A forked shader worker dies opening the compile cache](../reference/shaders.md#a-forked-shader-worker-dies-opening-the-compile-cache)
+- two builds of the same shaders give different `_bl` or `_bf` libraries for some keys, of the same size, 46 bytes apart, while their MSL is the same. A byte comparison of two library folders reports them, and `regress.py` can report them as changed variants. → [A library that depends on its source's file name](../reference/shaders.md#a-library-that-depends-on-its-sources-file-name)
+- the Mac or iOS shader step of a game with many pixel shaders spends most of its Metal compiles on libraries that few draws load: Pokémon Pokopia's whole map took 366,805 compiles, about 57% of them for `_ba` and `_bl`, and its runs opened none of the 5,775 of each. → [One compile for a pixel shader's side entry points](../reference/shaders.md#one-compile-for-a-pixel-shaders-side-entry-points)
+- the first Mac shader build of a title, with an empty output directory and CAS and no keys, takes tens of minutes while `J` Metal workers stay busy. On an Apple M2 MacBook Pro at `J=8`, Mario Kart 8 Deluxe took 22:46 (1366 s) and Super Mario Odyssey 30:02 (1802 s). → [Cold Mac shader builds compile each pixel shader more than once](../reference/shaders.md#cold-mac-shader-builds-compile-each-pixel-shader-more-than-once)
+- startup tasks can prepare the same shader more than once, even though only one object enters the warm dictionary. A measured Tears of the Kingdom startup avoided 320 duplicate preparations; this is not a steady-frame-rate claim. → [Concurrent warm tasks prepare each shader once](../reference/shaders.md#concurrent-warm-tasks-prepare-each-shader-once)
+- the Mac's pipeline prebuild (`DM_PSO_PREBUILD=tool`, the NSP script's `pso_prebuild.log`) runs on far fewer workers than the Mac has: `1 workers` on an M6, where Metroid Prime Remastered's 20,233 pairs took 1,725 s and Link's Awakening's 17,742 took 667 s, and 4 on an M5 Pro, where Donkey Kong Bananza's 21,225 took 6.5 to 7.5 minutes. → [A prebuild pool sized from the Super cores](../reference/shaders.md#a-prebuild-pool-sized-from-the-super-cores)
+- `fragmentFunction main0 cannot be used to build a pipeline state` (a Metal validation assertion). → [Function constants specialized at load](../reference/shaders.md#function-constants-specialized-at-load)
+- with Metal 4 pipelines on (the Mac; on iOS `pso_mtl4 on` or `DM_PSO_MTL4=1`), Apple's performance HUD says `Compiled 2 shaders. Total compilation time is 102.70ms. Consider adopting MTL4Compiler for more fine grained compilations` during play, when a shader with function constants loads. → [Functions with function constants specialized by the pipeline, not the library](../reference/shaders.md#functions-with-function-constants-specialized-by-the-pipeline-not-the-library)
+- a pipeline for a vertex shader that writes `render_target_array_index` cannot be built. → [Layer-writing vertex shaders get the triangle class](../reference/shaders.md#layer-writing-vertex-shaders-get-the-triangle-class)
+- the picture bright green and white from some scene on, in runs with pipelines compiled on demand (prewarm lists off or short) and in none with `DM_NVN_PREDICT=0`. → [The precompile look-ahead restores the scissor](../reference/shaders.md#the-precompile-look-ahead-restores-the-scissor)
+- a GPU frame capture (`DM_GPU_CAPTURE`, or Xcode with `MTL_CAPTURE_ENABLED=1`) fails with `Capturing Render pipeline specialization is not supported`, or the capture layer crashes on the pipeline archive; a capture of the `MTLDevice` is refused. → [GPU frame captures need pipelines built through the device](../reference/shaders.md#gpu-frame-captures-need-pipelines-built-through-the-device)
+- the first boot sat on the loading clock for about a minute, the CPU idle, with every file read done after 12 s. → [A cold Metal cache looks like a long load](../reference/shaders.md#a-cold-metal-cache-looks-like-a-long-load)
+- missing geometry or bright patches just after loading, which disappear as background pipeline compilation finishes. → [Native mesh draws omitted while their pipeline compiles](../reference/shaders.md#native-mesh-draws-omitted-while-their-pipeline-compiles)
+- hundreds of geometry raster passes in combat, despite support for batching consecutive geometry draws. → [Geometry runs across occlusion query boundaries](../reference/shaders.md#geometry-runs-across-occlusion-query-boundaries)
+- geometry preparation dominates rendering CPU even after geometry passes have been batched; primitive assembly and capture-index deduplication repeat for unchanged meshes. → [Prepared geometry indices reused after byte comparison](../reference/shaders.md#prepared-geometry-indices-reused-after-byte-comparison)
+- geometry vertex-capture passes consume substantial GPU time before geometry compute or depth rasterization. → [Geometry capture initializes only missing words](../reference/shaders.md#geometry-capture-initializes-only-missing-words)
+- Apple's performance HUD (`DM_METAL_HUD=1`) says `Compiled 2 shaders. Total compilation time is 102.70ms. Consider adopting MTL4Compiler for more fine grained compilations` during play, with a dip in the frame-rate graph at that moment, for example when a new room first issues a scissored clear. → [The host's own shaders compiled from source at run time](../reference/shaders.md#the-hosts-own-shaders-compiled-from-source-at-run-time)
+- a raw global memory read of a memory pool made after the shader's arguments were encoded gets 0, though the pool exists when the GPU runs it; a task shader that reads both ends of a range this way loops `(0 - 1) >> 5` times and hangs the GPU. The memory report's `raw pool table words at commit` line counts the words that were out of date. → [Raw pool table words that predate the pools](../reference/shaders.md#raw-pool-table-words-that-predate-the-pools)
+- integer divisions by a constant cost GPU time that the compiler should have folded away. The MSL holds calls such as `(spvMulExtended<_2, uint>(uint(_5), 16u))._m1`, and spirv-cross defines `spvMulExtended` as `[[clang::optnone]]`, so each product stays a call to an unoptimized function. → [Extended products behind spirv-cross's optnone helper](../reference/shaders.md#extended-products-behind-spirv-crosss-optnone-helper)
+- integer results in a shader come out wrong where the game's code relies on signed arithmetic wrapping: a packed field unpacked by hand, an overflow test, a sign extension. In Ocarina of Time, one section of an otherwise dark wall in the Deku Tree (Gohma's room) was lit brightly and shimmered while the camera moved. Only models drawn with task / mesh shaders were affected; the vertex-shader models beside them were lit correctly. → [Signed integer overflow in translated shaders](../reference/shaders.md#signed-integer-overflow-in-translated-shaders)
