+DM_GUEST_FILE_CHUNK	switch	`=<size>` (whole MB, K / M / G; default 1G, at least 64M, at most 1024 files): the size of each guest file; one file when it is not under the block's size. The stall at the system's periodic flush grows with the file (on a 16 GB M5 Mac up to 148 ms with one 20 GB file, 4 ms with 20 files of 1 GB)
+DM_GUEST_FILE_DIR	path	Folder for `DM_GUEST_FILE`'s files (default the user's temporary folder; the iOS app sets it to `NSTemporaryDirectory()`)
+DM_GUEST_FILE_MIN_FREE	switch	`=<size>` (whole MB, K / M / G; default 2G, `0` never): with `DM_GUEST_FILE=1`, the game is stopped (exit code 87) when the guest files' disk has less room than that: its free space less what the files' dirty pages may still take. When `f_bavail` alone is short it counts what the system can purge (`NSURLVolumeAvailableCapacityForImportantUsageKey`), as long as `f_bavail` keeps half the minimum
+DM_GUEST_FILE_PUNCH	switch	`0` keeps the disk blocks of guest memory the game gives back (with `DM_GUEST_FILE=1`); by default they are punched out (`F_PUNCHHOLE`, which also drops the pages): the heap's at once, the alias region's by a sweep every 10 s once they have stayed given back over a whole interval
+DM_GUEST_FILE_SCAN	diagnostic	`=1` (with `DM_GUEST_FILE=1`): every 30 s a `[mem] guest file scan` line, the dirty guest pages by region (heap, alias, stack, other) and how many of them read all 0
+DM_GUEST_SPAWN_TEST	diagnostic	`=1` runs a self-test before `nnMain`: a host-started guest thread does SDK malloc / free and locks an `nn::os` mutex, and the result is logged; `=2` also keeps a resident thread
+DM_HID_LOG	diagnostic	`1` logs the HID button usage number of each button pressed on a raw HID gamepad (`pad_hid_mac.h`), to write a `DM_HID_MAP`
+DM_HID_MAP	switch	`=<map>` such as `A=1,B=2,X=4,Y=5`: which HID button usage presses each Switch button on a raw HID gamepad (names A B X Y LS RS L R ZL ZR PLUS MINUS LEFT UP RIGHT DOWN; default 8BitDo's D-input numbering by position, `pad_hid.h`); Mac only
+DM_HID_PADS	switch	`0` stops reading raw HID gamepads that GameController does not list, such as an 8BitDo in its own mode (`pad_hid_mac.h`); Mac only
+DM_HITCH_DUMP	diagnostic	`=<ms>[:<s>]`: when no frame has been presented for that long (from s seconds on), logs every guest thread once per stall: wait, last SVC, pc, lr, frames and registers
+DM_HLECOUNT	diagnostic	`=<file>` (`%d` = process ID) writes at exit a TSV of how many times the guest called each HLE function, zero counts included
+DM_HOME_QUIT	player	`0` turns off quitting with the controller's Home button (Mac, on by default): held for 2 s it opens a quit prompt over the game, A quits as ⌘Q does, B or Home closes it (`home_quit.h`)
+DM_HOME_QUIT_CAPTURE	test	`=<folder>`: `tests/t_home_quit_mac.m` also draws the Home button's quit prompt and hold indicator into PNG files there
+DM_HOME_QUIT_TEXT	switch	The texts of the Home button's quit prompt in the player's language, one per line: the hold indicator, the title, the note, the quit and the cancel button; a front end such as NX2Apple sets it ([Starting a game](frontends.md#starting-a-game)), an empty or missing line keeps the English text
+DM_HTML	path	Folder of the offline HTML pages (default: `html` beside the RomFS)
+DM_ICON	path	`=<image>` gives `build_ipa.sh` another app icon (default the `icon.jpg` beside the RomFS)
+DM_IMPORTS	diagnostic	When set, logs each import the loader binds to a host function and each forced takeover
+DM_JCMS_LOG	diagnostic	`=1` logs the game's Joy-Con mouse sensor and pointer calls (JcmsState reads, Nmouse handles, states and positions), at most once a second each
+DM_JIT_CAPTURE	path	Folder the Mac host writes what a game's `nn::jit` plugin makes to, one `<key>.jit` per request, for `dm/tools/jit_capture.py`; the NSP script sets it
+DM_JIT_LOG	diagnostic	`1` logs every `nn::jit` `GenerateCode` request
+DM_JIT_MISS_DUMP	diagnostic	Folder for the input of each `nn::jit` request a served image lacks
+DM_JIT_SERVE	path	Folder of a `nn::jit` image (`jit.img`, `jit.tsv`, `jit.out`): the Mac serves requests from it as the iOS app does, and the plugin does not run
+DM_KBD	switch	`=<text>`: the system keyboard returns that text as OK without showing an input box; unset with no window, it returns cancel
+DM_KBD_PLAYER	switch	`own` makes the Mac keyboard player 1 on its own, with controllers from player 2 on (by default the first controller shares player 1 with the keyboard)
+DM_KEYS	switch	Scripted input: `<seconds or f<frame>>:<key>[+key][~hold or ~f<frames>][*count/interval],…` presses buttons, sticks, touches and `Shake` at those times; `=@<file>` reads the steps from the file
+DM_LANG	player	Game language as a language code (default `en-US`; the iOS app sets the one the IPA was packed with)
+DM_LAUNCHER	switch	`0` skips the iOS settings screen or the Mac settings window and starts the game at once, with the saved choices (on the Mac also `--no-launcher`)
+DM_LAUNCH_PARAM	path	File whose bytes `oe::TryPopLaunchParameter` returns once; `oe::ExecuteProgram` sets it for the program it starts; a relaunch removes it
+DM_LINK_VTICK	switch	`0` keeps the system's display-link rate on 144 or 165 Hz Mac screens; by default the link counts only the refreshes nearest a steady 1/60 s clock
+DM_LOADHASH	diagnostic	`=1` logs an address-independent SHA-256 per module after loading
+DM_LONGFRAME	diagnostic	`0` turns off the long-frame log (on by default), a line for a frame over 20 ms or with GPU time over 16.7 ms that is clearly above recent frames
+DM_MEASURE_LOCK	path	`=<file>`: `scripts/regress.py`'s default `--lock`, the measuring lock `scripts/bench.py --lock` shares: held only while a game runs, and the harness's shader builds pause while another process holds it
+DM_MEMSTAT	diagnostic	`=<tsv>` writes one line per second of memory accounting: footprint, Metal allocation and resident and charged memory by region class (guest, code, malloc, gpu, …)
+DM_MEMSTAT_DUMP	diagnostic	`=<seconds>`: with `DM_MEMSTAT`, at that second writes every VM region of 1 MiB or more to `<tsv>.regions`
+DM_MEM_CAP_GB	switch	`=<GB>`: the memory watchdog exits when resident memory exceeds that many GB (default 24 on the Mac, none on iOS; `0` turns it off)
+DM_METAL_CAPTURE	diagnostic	`=1` makes `build_ipa.sh` produce a build that can write GPU frame captures (`MetalCaptureEnabled` in `Info.plist`), for `DM_GPU_CAPTURE`
+DM_METAL_HUD	player	`1` shows Apple's Metal performance HUD over the game (frame rate, frame and GPU time, memory)
+DM_MIC	player	`1` offers the device's microphone to a game's audio input streams (nn::audio InputStream, 48 kHz mono; mic_apple.m): Ocarina of Time plays its ocarina by voice; off by default; launcher setting (iOS)
+DM_MOTION	switch	`0` ignores live motion: every six-axis sensor lies flat as before (a shake still plays), and neither controllers' motion sensors nor the phone's device motion are switched on (`hle_hid.c`, `motion_apple.h`, `DmGamepad.m`); the NSP script's `--no-motion` (`MOTION = False`) builds it in
+DM_MOTION_ACC_SIGN	switch	`-1` flips only GameController's acceleration, for a source that reports the specific force (+1 G up at rest) instead of the gravity direction (`motion_fusion.h`); phone and raw HID samples are already in the Switch frame
+DM_MOTION_AXES	switch	`=<map>` such as `x,-z,y`: maps Apple's controller axes to the Switch's, a signed permutation applied to acceleration and rotation alike, for a controller whose axes differ (default `x,y,z`; phone and raw HID samples are already in the Switch frame)
+DM_MOTION_GAIN	switch	`=<gain>`: how fast the orientation is pulled toward the measured gravity, in rad/s per unit error (default 0.5; `0` integrates the gyro alone)
+DM_MOTION_HID	switch	On the Mac, `0` disables the passive Switch Pro Controller HID motion fallback (`motion_pro_mac.h`); enabled by default when exactly one physical Pro and one GameController Pro can use the same player slot; usable GameController motion takes priority
+DM_MOTION_LOG	diagnostic	`=1` logs player 1's mapped motion sample once a second per source (phone / GameController / controller HID; acceleration in G, gyro in revolutions/s), for checking the axes: flat and still reads acc 0 0 -1
+DM_MOVIE_TRACE	diagnostic	When set, the movie decoder logs every call with its thread and decoder (by default the first 12 calls, then every 2^k-th)
+DM_MSL_DIR	path	Parent folder of the shader libraries: NVN loads from `b-<chain version>/` or the one file `b-<chain version>.img` beneath it, Vulkan from `vk/`, GL from `gl/`; the app and the Mac launcher set it
+DM_NDVE_ENGINE	switch	`0` runs nn::ndve's movie network (Ocarina of Time's title and opening movies) as the game's 12 translated dispatches after its colour conversion instead of the host's kernels (dm/gfx/msl/ndve_engine.metal, on by default: the same output, about half the frame's GPU time on an M4 Pro); `ab` alternates with DM_AB; `check` runs the game's dispatches and the host's network beside them and logs every 300 frames how many texels of the two differ. The host's kernels take only the model they were written for, bound by its shaders' keys and its weights' layout (docs/reference/shaders.md, The NDVE movie network by the host)
+DM_NICK	switch	The account nickname the game sees (default `Player`)
+DM_NRO	switch	`0` turns off loading of the NRO modules the game's NRR files register (on by default): every `nn::ro::LoadModule` then fails
+DM_NRO_CAPTURE	diagnostic	folder: a `nn::ro::LoadModule` with no rewritten copy writes the image to `<folder>/<SHA-256>.nro` and ends the run (the NSP script's capture of an NRO the game decodes at run time)
+DM_NRO_DIR	path	folder of NROs the shader split reads like `main`, and in which it finds a CRA archive's file table (the NSP script sets it for a captured NRO)
+DM_NVN_ALLOCPROF	diagnostic	`=1` charges Metal allocation growth to each replayed op type and function, and logs command allocator sizes and each new texture (serial, size, format, storage); `=2` also gives each main submission a fresh command allocator and reports command memory by operation, by encoder, by submission size and the indirect-count multi-draws encoded
+DM_NVN_API	switch	`=<major>.<minor>` overrides the NVN API version `nvnDeviceGetInteger` reports (by default decided by the SDK version or the profile's `device_api`: 55.15 for SDK 11 and later, 53.313 before)
+DM_NVN_BARRIER	switch	How draw passes are ordered: `full` (default) waits for all earlier GPU work, `game` only for the previous pass's fragment stage unless the game set a barrier, `ab` alternates with the `DM_AB` phases
+DM_NVN_BC_ALIAS	diagnostic	Set 0 to send copies to an uncompressed alias instead of its block-compressed storage, for an A/B check; default 1
+DM_NVN_BF_WANT	path	`=<file>`: appends the key of each fragment shader whose passes could not be merged for lack of a readable-tile version to that file, once per key
+DM_NVN_BLEND_ZERO	switch	`0` leaves a NaN or Inf colour under a zero blend weight to Apple's blender, which stores NaN; by default a pixel shader returns it as 0 where the draw's blend state gives its source colour a zero weight on a floating-point target, as NVIDIA's blender does with the option NVN's driver sets
+DM_NVN_BLIT_ORDER	diagnostic	`0` drops the barriers between dependent operations in the copy encoder, which reproduces the fountain's vertical stripes in Mario Wonder
+DM_NVN_BORDER_KEEP	diagnostic	1 keeps the border corrections in every pipeline, as before the DMSH_BORDER_FC specialization: a wrong border colour with the specialization, or its cost (ab with DM_AB)
+DM_NVN_CAPTURE	diagnostic	`=<frame>:1:<path>` writes that frame (from its first submit to its present) to one file: pool bytes, registries, program keys, the textures whose content lives only in Metal, each submission's records with pointers relocatable, and the presented frame's SHA-256 and pixels; `dm/gfx/tests/nvn_replay` replays it ([development.md](development.md#frame-capture-and-replay))
+DM_NVN_CBDUMP	diagnostic	`=<dir>`: saves each draw's whole uniform blocks as `F<frame>_p<pass>_st<stage>_cb<slot>_<address>.bin`, and the source bytes of buffer-to-3D-texture copies, with `DM_NVN_PASSDUMP`
+DM_NVN_CBDUMP_KEY	diagnostic	`=<shader key>`: limits `DM_NVN_CBDUMP` to draws using that shader; for a vertex shader key also saves the draw's vertex streams and index buffer
+DM_NVN_CBLOG	diagnostic	`=1` logs the command memory the game adds to command buffers (`AddCommandMemory`: the first 60, then every 500th) and the out-of-memory callbacks
+DM_NVN_CLEARLOG	diagnostic	`=1` logs the first 40 colour clears drawn as a triangle (inside a pass, with a partial channel mask or a partial scissor)
+DM_NVN_CMDSTAT	diagnostic	`=1` logs every 300 frames the command recording usage: bytes and records per frame, command memory segment sizes, out-of-memory callbacks, host segments, count and bytes per command type
+DM_NVN_COUNT	diagnostic	`1` counts every NVN call (the counting trampolines) and writes the per-frame call lines; off by default (on with a `DM_NVN_TSV` table that has call rows), the count is an atomic add all recording threads share
+DM_NVN_CPUPROF	diagnostic	1 records CPU wall/time/instruction totals by renderer operation; off by default.
+DM_NVN_CSH_PRESCAN	diagnostic	`0` runs the compute-hoist scan at every pass's first draw even when no dispatch follows (as before; for timing)
+DM_NVN_CS_HOIST	switch	`0` makes a compute dispatch between the draws of a pass end it (on by default: the dispatch runs ahead of the pass when that changes nothing the draws before it read)
+DM_NVN_CUT	switch	`=<draws>`: the replay commits its command buffer every that many draws, between passes (default 64; `0` one command buffer per submission, except the cut after a buffer-to-texture copy)
+DM_NVN_CUT_BUSY	switch	`=0`: a segment boundary commits whatever the GPU is doing (the default commits only when earlier work has reported back, keeping the boundary's GPU timestamp otherwise)
+DM_NVN_DEFER	switch	`0` turns off the deferred pass merging of the TBDR optimizations (which run only with `DM_NVN_TBDR=1`)
+DM_NVN_DEFER_AB	diagnostic	`=1` keeps the deferred pass merging (with `DM_NVN_TBDR=1`) off until the second screenshot has been written, then turns it on, to compare the pictures with and without it
+DM_NVN_DEPTHOVR	diagnostic	`=<fragment shader key prefix>,…`: those draws compare depth with `ALWAYS`; with `DM_NVN_HILITE`, tells "the depth test rejects every fragment" from "nothing rasterizes"
+DM_NVN_DIRECT_UPLOAD	switch	0 disables bounded direct RGBA16F array uploads and keeps staged copies; on by default for supported non-sparse textures.
+DM_NVN_DIRTYCHECK	diagnostic	`=1` fully hashes pool content on every use to find changes the dirty-page tracking missed
+DM_NVN_DISPATCH_OVERLAP	switch	`1` runs the game's compute dispatches with no barrier of its and nothing else between them side by side in the copy encoder, as NVN allows; `0` orders each after the one before (the default unless the profile sets dispatch_overlap: Ocarina of Time does)
+DM_NVN_DISPATCH_OWN	switch	`=1` gives every compute dispatch a compute encoder of its own with full barriers, as before (by default dispatches share the copy encoder); `DM_NVN_PASSGPU` does the same
+DM_NVN_DLSS_BILINEAR	diagnostic	`=1` draws every DLSS evaluation with the bilinear stand-in, to tell MetalFX's part in a GPU fault or stall
+DM_NVN_DUAL_WANT	path	`=<file>`: appends the key of each pixel shader a pipeline blends with its output 1 as the second source, while it has no dual-source entry point, to that file, once per key (the profile's `dual_want.tsv`)
+DM_NVN_ENC_LABEL	diagnostic	`=1` labels every render encoder `p<pass in frame> <width>x<height> <first 8 digits of the first draw's fragment key> d<draws>` for Instruments traces
+DM_NVN_ENC_THREAD	player	`1` runs the replay's Metal encoding on a thread of its own while the submit thread goes on translating; on by default on iOS (`0` turns it off), off on macOS
+DM_NVN_END_ALIAS	diagnostic	`1` puts the resource-alias flush back on every encoder's end barrier (each costs the command allocator about 100 KB; the start barriers carry it); `ab` alternates it with the `DM_AB` phases
+DM_NVN_FAMILY_NOCOMPRESS	switch	`0` lets textures of a resolution family (one storage, several sizes) use lossless compression again; on by default they are written uncompressed, as a size reading another's compressed blocks decodes garbage (ASTRAL CHAIN's glow after the pause menu)
+DM_NVN_FAST_WAKE	switch	`0` makes the game's `SyncWait`s wake on the commit feedback handler instead of the GPU's own signal of the fence (on by default)
+DM_NVN_FB_DEPTH	switch	`0` lets a draw that samples the depth attachment of its own render pass read it in the pass, as before (undefined on Apple's GPUs: compressed blocks of what the tiles stored so far); by default a draw that also writes depth or stencil reads a copy, reused until the game's next barrier, and one after a draw of its pass wrote depth ends the pass first
+DM_NVN_FB_SNAP	switch	Draws that sample a colour attachment of their own render pass: unset, those that write it (or follow a write in the pass) read a snapshot, reused until the game's next barrier; `0` none; `all` a fresh snapshot for every draw that samples one (slow, diagnostic, no tile reads)
+DM_NVN_FB_TILE	switch	`0` makes such draws that sample the attachment at their own pixel (a projective sample at the vertex shader's screen varying) read a snapshot too, instead of the tile
+DM_NVN_FLUSH_FILL	switch	`0` starts a new GPU texture in a CPU-writable pool as zeros; by default one whose storage the game flushed before its first use is filled from that storage once
+DM_NVN_FRAMES	diagnostic	`=<N>` prints the NVN call table frame by frame for the first N frames (default 3)
+DM_NVN_FUSE_CHECK	diagnostic	`=1` checks pass merging in the same frame: renders the merged passes again apart and logs the comparison of the attachments every time
+DM_NVN_GEOMETRY_COLOR	switch	full retains inactive geometry color attachments; default omits them only when attachment bounds and state prove equivalence.
+DM_NVN_GEOMETRY_COMPUTE	diagnostic	split ends the geometry compute encoder after each stage for comparison; default combines compatible stages.
+DM_NVN_GEOMETRY_CUT	switch	0 disables extra segment-cut checks at geometry encoder boundaries; default obeys the existing draw threshold and safety gates there.
+DM_NVN_GEOMETRY_INDEX_CACHE	switch	0 disables exact-byte CPU primitive-map caching; default bounds it to 32 MiB and 4096 entries.
+DM_NVN_GEOMETRY_PACK	diagnostic	legacy uses the serial block-prefix scan for comparison; default fuses small draws and scans large block totals in parallel.
+DM_NVN_GEOMETRY_QUERY_RUN	switch	0 prevents geometry runs across samples-passed query reports/resets; default retains independent captures while queries replay at their raster positions.
+DM_NVN_GEOMETRY_WAIT	diagnostic	always drains before indexed geometry preparation for comparison; default waits only for possible writes to CPU assembly inputs.
+DM_NVN_GEO_HOIST	switch	`0` draws each geometry draw's capture and kernel at its own place, ending the render pass; by default a run of lean geometry draws is prepared before the pass opens and only their rasters are drawn in it (`geo_run_walk`)
+DM_NVN_GEO_LEAN	switch	`0` draws passthrough geometry shaders through their full kernel and reader, as before; by default a lean kernel culls and the program's own vertex shader draws (`nvn_geometry.inc`)
+DM_NVN_GEO_MESH	switch	`0` draws passthrough geometry pairs that have a mesh library (`geo_mesh.tsv`) through the compute path; by default they are mesh draws in the render pass (`geo_mesh_draw`)
+DM_NVN_GEO_MESH_RUN	switch	`0` dispatches every mesh geometry draw on its own; by default consecutive mesh draws of one pair whose result does not depend on their order are drawn as one dispatch (`geo_mesh_take`)
+DM_NVN_GPUPROF	diagnostic	`=<f>`, `<f1>-<f2>` or `every:<n>`: logs the GPU start and duration of every encoder of those frames (`[gpuprof]` lines from Metal 4 counter heap timestamps)
+DM_NVN_GPU_STORAGE	switch	`0` stops what the GPU writes into a texture in storage the CPU can read (a GPU texture, or one uploaded from its storage) from being written into that storage on the GPU (on by default; `gsb_target`, `gsb_store`), to measure it
+DM_NVN_GPU_TS	switch	`submission` or `segment_gaps`: what timestamp reports record instead of the default `segment` (the GPU start / end of the segment holding each report, without the GPU's idle time between the submission's command buffers): one time for the whole submission, or segment times with that idle time
+DM_NVN_HILITE	diagnostic	`=<fragment shader key>,…`: those draws use a solid colour instead (magenta, green, cyan, yellow, blue, white in order), to tell wrong geometry from wrong shading
+DM_NVN_INDLOG	diagnostic	`=<n>`: the first n indirect draws (and those of pass-dumped frames) log their arguments at encode and as the GPU left them once the submission completed, with the first indices
+DM_NVN_IXCLOG	diagnostic	`=<n>` logs the first n indirect draw and dispatch conversions (record to arguments) once the GPU has run them
+DM_NVN_KEYDUMP	diagnostic	`=<fs key>[:n[:every]]`: for up to n draws of that fragment shader (default 6), logs the pass dump binding lines plus vertex formats, vertex streams and uniform values; meant for devices
+DM_NVN_LAZY_CLEAR	switch	`0` makes clears with no draw after them open a pass of their own, as before; by default they wait to become the load action of the next pass on that target
+DM_NVN_LOADPROBE	diagnostic	`=1`, `2` or `3`: attachments whose load is skipped are cleared to a sentinel colour (`3` NaN; `1` NaN for float formats) and the sentinel pixels left at pass end are counted (should be 0)
+DM_NVN_LOG	diagnostic	`=1` logs the creation of NVN objects
+DM_NVN_MAGSCAN	diagnostic	`=<fs key>[:every[:phase]]`: device self-check that reads each pass's colour attachments every `every` frames (default 30) and logs `[mag]` lines for solid magenta 16×16 blocks or non-finite pixels
+DM_NVN_PARTIAL_UPLOAD	switch	`0` reads a texture whole from its storage at every re-upload; by default a re-upload reads only the images (a layer's level) with a page written since the last upload and copies the others from the texture it replaces
+DM_NVN_PASSDUMP	diagnostic	`=<f>`, `<f1>-<f2>` or `every:<n>`: logs every render pass of those frames (attachments, clears, shader keys, bindings, draw state, presents, and the host function and line that ended each pass) for the `tbdr_*.py` tools
+DM_NVN_PASSDUMP_ALL	diagnostic	`=1` makes `DM_NVN_PASSDUMP` record every draw and binding change, not only the first per pass and stage, including copies and inline uniform updates
+DM_NVN_PASSGPU	diagnostic	`=<file>` writes the GPU time of each render and compute pass from stage-boundary timestamp counters (off under Metal 4); also gives each dispatch its own encoder
+DM_NVN_PASSGPU_SERIAL	diagnostic	`=1` makes each encoder on the main command buffer wait until all earlier work has finished, so `DM_NVN_PASSGPU` times read each pass's exclusive time (totals read high)
+DM_NVN_POOL_CHUNKS	switch	`0` wraps a memory pool of 512 MB or more whole in one buffer, as before; by default it is wrapped in overlapping 16 MB chunks made as draws reach them, so only the parts in use are wired
+DM_NVN_POOL_FIRST	diagnostic	`1` logs every first read through the pool table of a texture ID whose texture was not made yet (up to 32 a frame): what the read found (the ID's stand-in, or the dummy, which reads 0), the texture, its view and how many frames before it was registered; it also checks at each such read whether the storage the stand-in was made from has been written since. The totals go to the log every 300 frames without it (`pool stand-ins up to frame`)
+DM_NVN_POOL_SKIP_RETIRED	switch	`=1` / `=0` overrides the profile's `pool_skip_retired`: a pool table (handles traced to no slot) gives a retired texture ID that holds no view the dummy texture instead of a Metal texture made again from its storage
