+### Frame generation between held presents
+
+- **Symptom:** a game held at 30 fps on a 60 Hz display link shows each frame for two ticks; 60 fps doubles the CPU and GPU work, and on an iPhone the heat that comes with it (Ocarina of Time at Hyrule Castle reached thermal state 2 within 80 s at 60 fps).
+- **Fix:** frame generation (`DM_FRAMEGEN=1`, launcher: Frame generation, offered with the 30 fps frame rate; `nvn_mtl.m` `fg_on`, `fg_encode`, the display link's `g_fg_owe`). It runs on the [present snapshots](#present-snapshots-the-games-swapchain-texture-free-at-once), which it turns on with a ring of at least 3. After each present's copy, in the same command buffer on the game's queue, MetalFX's frame interpolator (`MTL4FXFrameInterpolator`) makes the frame halfway between the previous present's copy and this one. The display link shows it on the tick that would have shown the present, and the present's own picture half its interval later: the game does the work of 30 frames a second and the screen shows 60, for the interpolation's GPU time and half an interval (16.7 ms) more from the game's present to the screen.
+- **Inputs:** the presented picture alone, so it holds for every game and needs nothing from a profile. The motion is found from the two pictures (`dm/gfx/msl/fgmv.metal`: each 64x64 px tile searched within +-192 px at 1/8 size, then each 8 px block given its tile's, a neighbour's or no motion, whichever matches best around it), as MetalFX's own flow tore or gave up past about 96-192 px a frame at 1176x540; depth is constant. Still detailed pixels, a HUD, are taken from the present itself after the interpolation (`fg_keep`), as MetalFX moved a still HUD beside moving pixels even with its own vectors at zero. `DM_FRAMEGEN_MV=0` gives no motion.
+- **Size:** a picture shown smaller than the game draws it is copied at the size shown, and a present's crop alone (Ocarina of Time draws 2352x1080 into a 3136x1440 texture), so the interpolation, whose cost follows its pixels (on an M5 about 4 ms at 1920x1080, 5-7 ms at 2560x1440 and 3136x1440), works on what is shown. **Frame generation size** (`DM_FRAMEGEN_SCALE`, Full or Lighter = 0.75) makes the copy smaller still, and the present's MetalFX spatial scaler, turned on for it, brings each present back.
+- **Pacing:** generated frames only for presents held 1.5 ticks or more, on the display link only. A present that does not follow the one before starts the interpolator's history again; its first two outputs are the new frame unchanged, so generated frames are shown from the third on. A slot is reused once the present after its own has been processed. The picture shown after its generated frame takes a present allocator of its own (3 to 5), so the display link thread does not wait for the GPU to run the generated frame's present. The display link does not commit the residency set for the host's own snapshots and generated frames (in their own set): that commit takes the lock a submitting thread holds while it commits a frame's work, and callbacks of 59-138 ms on an iPhone were hitches. With frame generation on, the 30 fps cap keeps the link at 60 Hz.
+- **Drops:** frame generation never falls back to the game's 30 fps. A present the display link had to wait for still shows its generated frame first; only one taken over older ones dropped for it shows its own picture, that present alone. While frames keep dropping (a drop within 30 presents of the one before), the interpolation is made lighter instead of stopped: its pixel budget steps from none (the picture as shown, Wide view's too, as X9) to 1920x1080, 1440x810, 1200x675 and 960x540 (a step that would not make the picture smaller is passed over) (never below half the size shown), and past the last step it stays there and keeps running. A hitch (a present three intervals or more after the one before: a load, a scene change) is not counted toward a step. A step back up after 600 presents in a row in time (20 s), twice that (up to 7,200) when a step up was followed by a step down within 600 presents. No patches of the present are laid over the generated frame where its motion is not trusted (X13-FF3 did, and they showed as blocks on camera turns); only the still HUD is kept. FF2 stopped the interpolation instead, and the picture switched between 60 and 30 eight times in 90 s (log 17). Decided by dropped frames, not by temperature. Encoding on its own thread (`DM_NVN_ENC_THREAD`, on by default on iOS) matters here: with it off, an iPhone's submit thread took 10-28 ms a frame and frames came late.
+- **Diagnostics:** the interpolation's GPU time every 600 generated frames (between precise timestamps around it), counts of generated frames, drops and budget steps every 1,800, and a line for any display link callback of 50 ms or more with where its time went.
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/gfx/tests GAME=0400486024DEC000 check_t_framegen`: real presents through an offscreen layer and the display link's callback called tick by tick. A square moving between frames comes out halfway and a still one stays; the order generated frame, own picture, held; pauses and the stop and resume while frames drop; crops and smaller screens; the picture after a generated frame does not wait while the GPU is held; and a run at `DM_FRAMEGEN_SCALE=0.5`.
+- **Found on:** [Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) (iPhone 18 Pro: 30 fps shown at 60 with 0-1 of 300 frames dropped and the interpolation about 10 ms a frame at 1920x1080; Mac, M5).
+- **Not checked:** other games; the Mac's picture in play.
+
 ### Vulkan acquire and vsync event follow the display link
 
 - **Symptom:** a steady game whose frame-rate counter wanders between 50 and 60 on the iPhone only; the `display link up to tick` lines show about as many empty ticks as frames dropped by merging: frames arrive two in one tick and none in the next.
